<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="Mixed Workload — Load (stepped capacity verification)" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Load test for the mixed-workload boundary discovered by the stress run.
All four endpoints fire concurrently, sharing one Prisma pool, one
Postgres lock manager, one fraud detector pipeline.

╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ BOUNDARY (from mixed-workload stress run): 182 total VUs             ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Composition (preserving the 10:1:1:2 ratio used in the stress run):  ║
║                                                                      ║
║   Endpoint        VUs    Share of mix                                ║
║   ───────────────────────────────────                                ║
║   Account read   130     ~71%                                        ║
║   Deposit         13     ~7%                                         ║
║   Withdraw        13     ~7%                                         ║
║   Transfer        26     ~14%                                        ║
║   ───────────────────────────────────                                ║
║   Total          182                                                 ║
║                                                                      ║
║ At t=6:30 of the stress run, 182 active VUs (split per the ratio     ║
║ above) ran fully clean (Avg 16ms, 0% errors). The next step at       ║
║ ~196 VUs (~7 min into the ramp) produced 7.89% errors. So 182 is     ║
║ the last fully clean MIXED step.                                     ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Brackets follow the ±~5% convention from the per-endpoint load       ║
║ plans. Per-TG numbers are rounded to whole VUs while preserving      ║
║ the overall total at each phase:                                     ║
║                                                                      ║
║   Phase          Total   Read   Dep   With   Trans   Maps to         ║
║   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────      ║
║   warm-up         127     91     9     9      18    70% (recovery)   ║
║   just-under      173    124    12    12      25    95% of boundary  ║
║   at boundary     182    130    13    13      26    100% (10-min)    ║
║   just-over       192    137    14    14      27    105%             ║
║   recovery        127     91     9     9      18    70%              ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

What this test answers:
  - Does the mixed boundary HOLD over sustained time (not just for the
    30s the stress run measured at each step)?
  - Does the cross-endpoint interaction degrade slowly, or stay flat?
  - Does read p95 stay near its isolated baseline (~17ms) while the
    writes are at their share of the mix?

This is a HYBRID stepped capacity scan with recovery — not a single-
level steady-state load test. Each step holds long enough to expose
slow degradation (fraud_signals table growth, transaction history
accumulation, Postgres query-plan drift, connection-pool dynamics).

Each TG ramps its own threads through the same 5-phase schedule.
At any moment the four TGs are AT THE SAME RELATIVE PHASE (warm-up /
just-under / at-boundary / just-over / recovery). The Aggregate
Report breaks down per-sampler so each endpoint's metrics are
visible independently.

Shape (Ultimate Thread Group per TG, 28 min total):

  ┌──────────────┬─────────────┬───────┬─────────────────────────┐
  │ Window       │ Total VUs   │ Hold  │ Maps to spec            │
  ├──────────────┼─────────────┼───────┼─────────────────────────┤
  │ 0–5 min      │ 127         │  5 m  │ 70%  (warm-up)          │
  │ 5–10 min     │ 173         │  5 m  │ 95%  (just under)       │
  │ 10–20 min    │ 182         │ 10 m  │ 100% (at boundary)      │
  │ 20–25 min    │ 192         │  5 m  │ 105% (just over)        │
  │ 25–28 min    │ 127         │  3 m  │ 70%  (recovery)         │
  └──────────────┴─────────────┴───────┴─────────────────────────┘

Pass criteria (per endpoint — measured independently in the
Aggregate Report):

  ┌────────────────┬───────────┬───────────────────┐
  │ Endpoint       │ p95       │ Error rate budget │
  ├────────────────┼───────────┼───────────────────┤
  │ Account read   │  &lt; 200ms  │  &lt; 1%             │  ← the SLA
  │ Deposit        │  &lt; 5000ms │  &lt; 5%             │
  │ Withdraw       │  &lt; 5000ms │  &lt; 5%             │
  │ Transfer       │  &lt; 5000ms │  &lt; 10%            │  ← heaviest
  └────────────────┴───────────┴───────────────────┘

Response assertions:
  - Read     : exactly 200
  - Deposit  : ^(2\d\d|422)$ (2xx or 422 business rejection)
  - Withdraw : ^(2\d\d|422)$
  - Transfer : ^(2\d\d|422)$

Think time: Gaussian Random Timer on EACH thread group (mean 3s,
σ 1s). Same realistic-pacing model as the per-endpoint load tests.

Amounts (chosen to avoid fraud signals that would distort metrics):
  - Deposit:  $100.00      (deposits aren't debits — no fraud check)
  - Withdraw: $50.25       (non-round, &lt;$3k — avoids ROUND_AMOUNT
                            and LARGE_AMOUNT_SINGLE)
  - Transfer: $0.10        (smallest meaningful — below all limits)

⚠ Weekend caveat: transfer TG bypasses lock+update+insert path on
Sat/Sun UTC (PENDING_WEEKEND queuing). Run on a weekday.

Load-generator memory: 192 JVM threads × ~1 MB stack each ≈ 192 MB
peak. Default JMeter heap (1 GB) is plenty.

Run headless from PROJECT ROOT:
  jmeter -n -t tests/stress/jmeter/load/mixed-workload.load.jmx \
         -l tests/stress/reports/mixed-workload-load.jtl \
         -e -o tests/stress/reports/mixed-workload-load-html

Or via the wrapper script:
  npm run test:load:mixed

Prereqs:
  1. npx tsx tests/stress/scripts/seed.ts  (accounts.csv + transfers.csv)
  2. Server running on localhost:3000 (npm run build &amp;&amp; npm start)
  3. JMeter Plugin: jpgc-casutg (Custom Thread Groups)</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
